Below is the exact HTTP request you would send to VirusTotal to retrieve the **sub‑domain** related objects for `example.org`.  
Only the object IDs and any `context_attributes` will be returned in the response (the rest of the attributes are omitted).

```http
GET https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related_objects?relationship=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc
Host: www.virustotal.com
x-apikey: my_api
Accept: application/json
```

---

### Expected JSON response format

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "subdomain1.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "context_attributes": {
          /* any context attributes that apply to this sub‑domain */
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "subdomain2.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "context_attributes": {
          /* any context attributes that apply to this sub‑domain */
        }
      }
    }
    /* up to 10 objects, depending on the cursor */
  ],
  "meta": {
    "continuation_cursor": "next_cursor_value_if_more_results"
  }
}
```

> **Note:**  
> - The `context_attributes` field may be empty or omitted if no context data is available for a particular sub‑domain.  
> - The `continuation_cursor` in the `meta` section will contain the next cursor value if there are more results beyond the 10 requested.  

If you actually execute the request above with your API key (`my_api`), you’ll receive a JSON payload similar to the structure shown, containing only the IDs and any context attributes for each sub‑domain.
